      "math_academy_id": "e-4140",
      "problem": "What is the inverse of $f(x) = 2\\log_4(3x)-1?$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3x)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3y)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3y)-1 \\\\ 2{log}_{4}⁡(3y) & =x+1 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3y)} & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ 3y & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}{4}^{(x+1)/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) =\\dfrac13 4^{(x+1)/2}.$",
